      "fn": false,
      "quote": "Human relationships are vast as deserts: they demand all daring.",
      "attribution": "Patrick White",
      "source": "Voss, 1957",
      "description": "The Red Centre (Uluru and the outback) is the spiritual void and the physical core of the continent. It is the empty space that haunts the coastal fringe, a place of silence, red earth, and ancient rock. For the explorer (like Voss), it is a place of metaphysical trial; for the tourist, a bucket list item; for the Indigenous, the center of the Dreaming.",
      "establishes": "This establishes The Red Centre as the Void. It is the silence at the center of the noise, the \"Dead Heart\" that is paradoxically full of life. Geographically, Australia has a hollow center; culturally, this creates a \"horror vacui\" that pushes the population to the edge. It stands as the ultimate judge of authenticity—only those who can survive the Centre truly belong to the land. It represents the unknowable god of the Australian landscape, indifferent and sublime. It is the gravitational well that pulls the imagination inwards.",
