      "name": "The Tyranny of Distance",
      "cleanName": "The Tyranny of Distance",
      "role": "Constraint",
      "fn": false,
      "quote": "The Antipodes were drifting, though where they were drifting no one knew.",
      "attribution": "Geoffrey Blainey",
      "source": "The Tyranny of Distance, 1966",
      "description": "Distance is the central fact of Australian existence—distance from the motherland (UK), distance from markets, and distance between cities. It has shaped everything from the cost of goods to the psychological feeling of isolation (\"The Cultural Cringe\"). It turns the simple act of travel or communication into a significant logistical feat.",
      "establishes": "This establishes Distance as the Constraint. It is the friction coefficient of the entire system, taxing every interaction with time and cost. It forces a reliance on state intervention (to build railways/telegraphs) because private enterprise cannot bridge the gap profitable. It creates a deep anxiety about being \"left behind\" or \"forgotten\" by the world, driving the \"Punching Above Weight\" syndrome. It turns communication into a survival trait, binding the isolated communities together. It defines the \"Where\" as \"Far Away.\"",
      "coreMeaning": "It is the friction coefficient of the entire system, taxing every interaction with time and cost.",
      "coords": {
        "v": 0.0,
        "psi": -0.6
      },
      "zone": "Constraint",
      "rationale": "Neither good nor evil, but imposes friction. Drives connectivity efforts.",
      "hansonVerdict": "HIT",
      "alboVerdict": "HIT",
      "inHanson353": true,
      "qA": "We should respect and adapt wisely to the continental reality of The Tyranny of Distance (Constraint): it is the friction coefficient of the entire system, taxing every interaction with time and cost.",
