      "description": "D.H. Lawrence, the English novelist, wrote this observation in his 1923 novel Kangaroo after living in Thirroul, NSW, where he was confronted by the radical egalitarianism of Australian men. He was struck by the absence of the deferential \"cap-doffing\" class structure he knew in England, noting that the Australian working man felt no innate inferiority to anyone. \"Demos\" (the people) being \"his own master\" refers to a society where the common man assumes he is the highest authority.",
