      "source": "Preface to Gordon's Poems, 1876",
      "description": "Marcus Clarke, the great colonial novelist, wrote this famous essay attempting to capture why the Australian bush felt so different to the European forest. He identified its \"timelessness\" and its \"monstrosities\" (strange flora/fauna) as evoking a specific mood of \"Weird Melancholy\" rather than traditional beauty. He argued that the scribblings of nature here were \"hieroglyphs\" that the European mind could not yet read, creating a sense of isolation and reflection.",
