      "attribution": "Henry Lawson",
      "source": "To Be Amused, 1899",
      "description": "Lawson's poem captures the central tragedy of the Australian climate: the precious water that \"runs to sea\" (mud) while the land thirsts. He describes the \"haggard eyes\" of the settler staring at the sky, waiting for rain that doesn't come. It articulates the deep psychological anxiety of a people living on the driest inhabited continent, where \"The Thirst\" is a permanent condition.",
