      "attribution": "Henry Lawson",
      "source": "While the Billy Boils, 1896",
      "description": "The Battler is the moral agent of hardship. Unlike the \"victim,\" the Battler fights back, usually against \"The Banks,\" \"The Government,\" or \"The Drought.\" In the 1990s, John Howard weaponized this archetype (\"Howard's Battlers\") to capture the working-class vote, framing them as the \"forgotten people\" of the suburbs. Use of the term implies a person who is losing, but refuses to surrender.",
