      "attribution": "Cultural Imperative",
      "source": "Social Norm",
      "description": "Australians are rigid queuers, treating the line as a sacred geometry of fairness. While the \"Pub\" is a chaotic scramble, the \"Queue\" is the absolute rule of civil society. The idiomatic \"First in, best dressed\" establishes that priority is determined solely by timing, not by status, wealth, or class. Jumping the queue is viewed not as a minor rudeness, but as a fundamental violation of the \"Fair Go.\"",
      "establishes": "This establishes Queuing as the Order. It enacts the \"Egalitarian Line\" where every citizen is equal before the service. Status cannot buy a place at the front; only \"Time\" (the willingness to wait) can purchase priority. It is the method by which a potentially anarchic society organizes access to resources without resorting to violence or aristocracy. It proves that the Australian respects the \"Process\" more than the \"Person.\"",
