      "attribution": "Ellis Ashmead-Bartlett",
      "source": "Gallipoli Dispatch, 1915",
      "description": "The Great War was the \"Sanguinary Baptism\" of the nation. It was the event that supposedly \"made\" Australia, proving to the world that the \"Colonial Boy\" could fight as well as the \"Imperial Soldier.\" Ashmead-Bartlett's famous dispatch described the Anzacs as a superior \"race of athletes,\" validating the entire colonial project. The blood sacrifice proved the \"breed\" was not degenerate.",
      "establishes": "This establishes The War as the Validator. It provides the \"Sacred Myth\" (Anzac). It drives the \"Service\" ethic and the reverence for the \"Fallen.\" It proves that the Australian drive is capable of ultimate self-abnegation for a cause, refuting the charge of purely selfish materialism. It binds the generations in a blood pact of remembrance, ensuring every April 25th the nation re-affirms its core identity through loss. It converted shame (convict) into pride (soldier).",
      "coreMeaning": "It provides the \"Sacred Myth\" (Anzac). It drives the \"Service\" ethic and the reverence for the \"Fallen.\" It proves that the Australian drive is capable of ultimate self-abnegation for a cause, refuting the charge of purely selfish materialism.",
