      "cleanName": "Resistance",
      "role": "Resistance",
      "fn": true,
      "quote": "We are the survivors of a war that has never ended.",
      "attribution": "Isabel Coe",
      "source": "Aboriginal Tent Embassy, 1990s",
      "description": "The official narrative treats World War I as the nation's \"Baptism of Fire,\" forging identity on the foreign shores of Gallipoli. This ignores the 140-year Frontier War that was fought on Australian soil to secure the land itself. The \"Fallen\" includes tens of thousands of warriors like Pemulwuy and Jandamarra who died defending their country against invasion long before 1915.",
      "establishes": "This establishes Resistance as the First War. The Anzac legend celebrates fighting for Empire overseas; the Frontier War acknowledges the fight for Country at home. Coe's statement reminds the nation that the \"peace\" is merely an \"occupation.\" It validates the warrior tradition that defended the continent against the \"Digger's\" ancestors.",
      "coreMeaning": "The Anzac legend celebrates fighting for Empire overseas; the Frontier War acknowledges the fight for Country at home.",
