      "source": "H.C. Coombs, (Director of Rationing, 1944)",
      "description": "The Public Servant exists in the perpetual tension between the \"Guardian\" and the \"Stifler,\" a duality that defines the Australian State. H.C. \"Nugget\" Coombs articulated the noble ideal during World War II, framing the bureaucrat not as a paper-pusher but as a moral shield, enforcing the \"Ration\" to protect the vulnerable from the predatory greed of the black market.",
      "establishes": "This establishes The Public Servant as the Moral Architect of Mediocrity. While the original intent was to create a \"fair share\" for all, Robin Boyd's critique reveals that this protective instinct inevitably ossifies into the \"Old Man\"—a timid, grey bureaucracy that prioritizes safety over excellence and regulation over beauty. The agent becomes a biological brake on the nation, ensuring that no individual rises too high above the \"Standard,\" thereby protecting the collective from risk but strangling the culture of greatness.",
