      "attribution": "Mary Durack",
      "source": "Kings in Grass Castles, 1959",
      "description": "\"Yakka\" comes from \"yaga,\" meaning work in the Yagara Aboriginal language of southern Queensland—the First Australians named the concept before the settlers arrived. \"Hard Yakka\" became the philosophy of strenuous effort: the belief that value comes through sweat, that the land does not yield to wishes but only to relentless labor. Mary Durack's pastoral saga captured the generational arc: those who built Australia through hard yakka so their children wouldn't have to.",
      "establishes": "This establishes Hard Yakka as the Purpose Principle. It defines the Australian method's \"Why\" as fundamentally physical: we do things this way because the land demands effort, not elegance. It grounds the national philosophy in Indigenous etymology, acknowledging that the work ethic predates European arrival. It justifies the cultural suspicion of \"easy money\" or \"soft hands\"—those who haven't done the hard yakka haven't earned their place. The purpose of method is not beauty but sweat.",
