      "quote": "She'll be right.",
      "attribution": "",
      "source": "Cultural Idiom, (Mid-20th Century)",
      "description": "Australian Pragmatism oscillates between the Folk Optimism of \"She'll be right\" and the State Realism of \"Mutual Concessions.\" Alfred Deakin elevated this logic to a political art form with the \"Acceptable Constitution,\" a cobbling together of contradictory US and UK systems to forge a nation, agreeing that \"Perfection\" is the enemy of \"Function.\"",
      "establishes": "This establishes Pragmatism as the Logic of Sufficient Outcome. The Australian method judges a system not by its internal consistency or philosophical purity, but by whether it survives the night. \"She'll be right\" is not just laziness; it is a calculation of risk and energy, an optimistic fatalism born of the need to keep moving in a harsh environment. It confirms that the national why is \"To Work,\" not \"To Be True,\" creating a culture that prefers a functioning patch to a broken ideal.",
      "coreMeaning": "The Australian method judges a system not by its internal consistency or philosophical purity, but by whether it survives the night.",
