      "quote": "The roster system of engaging labor.",
      "attribution": "",
      "source": "Waterside Workers, (1917)",
      "description": "The Roster is the colonization of Time, the mechanism by which the Australian worker sells their life structure. It began with the Wharfies as a tool of fairness—replacing the \"Bull Pick\" (Favoritism) with a computerized list to ensure every man got a turn—and evolved into the FIFO Roster, which slices a human life into \"Production Time\" (On Swing) and \"Consumption Time\" (Off Swing).",
      "establishes": "This establishes The Roster as the Industrial Clock of the Soul. It represents the Australian willingness to submit to rigid \"Time-Discipline\" in exchange for high wages and fairness, turning the calendar itself into an industrial instrument. The Roster allows the worker to endure the \"Camp\" or the \"Wharf\" by promising a defined \"Escape,\" proving that the Australian method manages the misery of labor not by improving the condition, but by strictly bounding the duration.",
