      "role": "The Shortcut",
      "fn": false,
      "quote": "Near enough is good enough.",
      "attribution": "",
      "source": "Cultural Idiom,",
      "description": "The Shortcut is the Shadow of the Bricoleur, where improvisation degenerates into laziness. \"Near Enough\" is the dark side of \"She'll be right\"—it accepts mediocrity as a standard because the effort to achieve excellence is deemed unnecessary, a mindset Robin Boyd critiqued as creating a nation of \"Veneer\" and \"Featurism.\"",
      "establishes": "This establishes The Shortcut as the Enemy of Excellence. It defines a culture that prioritizes the \"Done\" over the \"Beautiful\" and the \"Quick\" over the \"Lasting.\" The Shortcut is embedded in the national DNA as a \"Quality-Killer,\" born from the colonial habit of building temporary structures for a temporary stay. It proves that while the Bricoleur can save the machine, the Shortcut ensures the machine will break again, creating a cycle of \"Good Enough\" repairs that prevents true permanent greatness.",
      "coreMeaning": "It defines a culture that prioritizes the \"Done\" over the \"Beautiful\" and the \"Quick\" over the \"Lasting.\" The Shortcut is embedded in the national DNA as a \"Quality-Killer,\" born from the colonial habit of building temporary structures for a temporary stay.",
