      "role": "Usurpation",
      "fn": false,
      "quote": "The squatter saw the sections, and his eye with joy did shine / 'I'll have all the land,' he said, 'from here to the border line.'",
      "attribution": "",
      "source": "The Squatter's Man, anonymous colonial bush ballad, collected c. 1860s",
      "description": "This folk satire captured the widespread resentment of the pastoralists who claimed millions of acres beyond the official \"Limits of Location,\" forcing the Crown to legalize their usurpation after the fact. The Squatter was the lawless pastoralist who simply took the land beyond the official Limits of Location, driving sheep into the interior and claiming vast runs by right of occupation. They were technically criminals (hence \"squatters\"), but their economic power forced the government to recognize their claims retrospectively. They became the Aristocracy.",
      "establishes": "This establishes Usurpation as the economic drive of the Cause. The \"Who\" who built the wealth did so by taking more than they were given and then demanding the law catch up to them. The Squatter embodies the \"Greater Lie\" of the Australian economy: claim first, ask permission later. They created the Pastoral Aristocracy that would dominate politics for a century, proving that in the colony, possession was not nine-tenths of the law—it was the law. The rich made themselves rich by ignoring the rules.",
