      "attribution": "Arthur Calwell, Minister for Immigration",
      "source": "House of Representatives Hansard, 2 December 1947",
      "description": "Defending the deportation of wartime Chinese refugees despite their Australian families, Calwell, architect of post-war immigration, remained an unapologetic defender of the White Australia Policy even as he welcomed millions of European migrants. Calwell's infamous comment encapsulates the original immigration policy: Australia was for the British, and everyone else was kept out. The \"Effect\" of the founding was a racially homogenous population designed to remain permanently so. The Immigration Restriction Act of 1901 was the first act of the new Parliament—a statement of intent from Day One.",
