      "fn": false,
      "quote": "Whereas the people of New South Wales, Victoria, South Australia, Queensland, and Tasmania, humbly relying on the blessing of Almighty God, have agreed to unite in one indissoluble Federal Commonwealth under the Crown.",
      "attribution": "Preamble to the Commonwealth of Australia Constitution Act 1900",
      "source": "",
      "description": "(63 & 64 Victoria, c. 12), Royal Assent 9 July 1900, proclaimed 1 January 1901. Negotiated over a decade of conventions and ratified by colonial referendums, it is a technocratic document with no Bill of Rights. Unlike the American Declaration, born in revolution, the Australian Constitution was negotiated over a decade of polite conferences and ratified by referendums. It is a pragmatic, technocratic document concerned with tariffs and railways, not ringing declarations of rights. It has no Bill of Rights.",
      "establishes": "This establishes Pragmatism as the definition of the Rule. The \"What\" is a mechanism, not a poem. The Constitution is a plumber's manual, not a Sermon on the Mount. It tells you how the pipes work, not why they matter. This reflects the deep Australian suspicion of grand claims and high rhetoric. We prefer the thing that works to the thing that inspires. The nation is defined by process, not passion.",
