      "description": "After decades of asking tourists to respect their wishes, the traditional owners finally closed the climb permanently, an act of sovereignty over a site that had been \"discovered\" by Europeans only in 1873. The monolith in the center of the continent is the most sacred site of the Anangu people, representing the deep-time spiritual connection between the land and its original custodians. The decision to close the climb in 2019 was a symbolic act of returning the \"Where\" to its rightful owners.",
      "establishes": "This establishes Sacredness as the contested geography of the Cause. The \"Where\" is not just dirt; it is a Cathedral of the Spirit, belonging to those who have sung the Songlines for millennia. For the settler, Uluru is a tourist destination; for the Indigenous, it is a Church. The return of the rock is a small act of Reconciliation, acknowledging that the foundation was built on someone else's holy ground. The Center is not empty; it is full of meaning we cannot read.",
