      "source": "Farewell Address, Sydney, 1821",
      "description": "Macquarie championed the \"Emancipist\" policy, allowing convicts who had served their sentence to own land, hold office, and become full citizens—a revolutionary assertion that the Stain could be washed away by honest labor and time. Macquarie believed that a convict who had served their time should be allowed to become a full citizen, owning land and holding office. This \"Emancipist\" policy was revolutionary, asserting that the Stain could be washed away by honest labor. It created the possibility of a \"Second Chance.\"",
